{
 "title": "The Behavior of Inventories over the Business Cycle: Evidence across Levels of Development",
 "hypothesis": "Inventories are strongly procyclical in advanced economies but become acyclical at lower income levels, while volatility and persistence rise as development declines; productivity shocks generate temporary accumulation and financial shocks generate decumulation-rebound in higher-income groups but delayed unreversed decline in low-income economies.",
 "data_sources": [
  "World Bank World Development Indicators national accounts 1993-2022 for changes in inventories, GDP, investment, government consumption, imports, exports, population; implicit GDP deflator for deflation; Federal funds futures FF4 monetary surprises; Ludvigson et al macro-financial uncertainty index; U.S. BAA corporate bond yield and U.S. Treasury yield spread"
 ],
 "sample": "Balanced panel of small open economies with complete records for changes in inventories, GDP, investment, government consumption, imports, exports and population over 1993-2022. Excludes countries with GDP >2% of world GDP (Germany, United States, France, Italy, Japan, United Kingdom) and countries with recurrent institutional instability or constituted after 1993 (Mauritania, Slovakia, Togo, North Macedonia). Changes in inventories defined per SNA 1993/2008 as net variation in goods held including additions, withdrawals and recurrent losses, component of gross capital formation. Final panel 72 countries classified by IMF (advanced vs emerging) and World Bank income mode 1993-2022: 24 advanced high-income, 14 high and upper-middle-income emerging, 24 lower-middle-income emerging, 10 low-income emerging.",
 "period_start": "1993",
 "period_end": "2022",
 "method": [
  "Step 1 - Deflate changes in inventories: Delta INV = Delta INV_curr / implicit GDP price index, normalize as Delta inv_t / Y_trend,t-1 where Y_trend is GDP at constant prices trend from log-quadratic filter. Use one-period lag of trend to reduce endogeneity per Uribe 2017 variant.",
  "Step 2 - Extract cyclical component: Estimate country-specific quadratic filter in levels: Delta inv_t / Y_trend,t-1 = beta0,i + beta1,i*t + beta2,i*t^2 + epsilon_i,t. Residual epsilon_i,t = Delta inv_t^c. Expenditure and trade aggregates expressed in per capita logs, first-differenced to obtain cyclical growth rates.",
  "Step 3 - Compute stylized facts by country-group medians: Pearson correlations between Delta inv_t^c and Delta y,c,i,g,x,m; standard deviations absolute volatility and ratios sigma(Delta inv^c)/sigma(Delta y etc); first-order autocorrelation Corr(Delta inv_t^c; Delta inv_{t-1}^c).",
  "Step 4 - Identify long-run productivity shock via KPSW long-run restriction: Vector X_i,t = [y^g,c,i]' in per capita logs where y^g = GDP excluding government consumption. Cointegration rank 2 with beta' = [-1 1 0; -1 0 1] so cointegrating vectors c-y^g and i-y^g stationary. Panel VECM with country fixed effects mu_i, pooled adjustment alpha and short-run Gamma: Delta X_i,t = mu_i + alpha beta' X_{i,t-1} + sum_{j=1}^p Gamma_j Delta X_{i,t-j} + u_i,t, p=1 baseline. C(1) = beta_perp [ alpha_perp' (I - sum Gamma_j) beta_perp ]^{-1} alpha_perp'. Shock shock^PS_i,t = e_y' C(1) u_i,t with e_y'=[1 0 0], sign normalized to positive long-run effect on output. Test persistence via AR(1) with country FE and country-clustered SE: shock_t on shock_{t-1}.",
  "Step 5 - Identify financial shock via LP-IV: BAA_t = yield Baa corporate - Treasury risk-free. Instrument with Federal Reserve monetary surprises SM_t = sum_{j in t} FF4_j (high-frequency 3-month fed funds futures around FOMC) aggregated annual sum, and global macro-financial uncertainty UNC_t = annual arithmetic mean of monthly Ludvigson et al index. First stage BAA_t = gamma0 + gamma1 SM_t + gamma2 UNC_t + nu_t on annual global series 1993-2022 (30 years). Financial shock = instrumented component hat(BAA)_t. Evaluate instrument relevance via first-stage F and Montiel Olea-Pflueger effective F by group/horizon clustering by year after partialling country FE, overidentification Hansen J, endogeneity Durbin-Wu-Hausman, persistence AR(1) as above.",
  "Step 6 - Local projections Jorda for horizons h=0..5: Delta inv^c_{i,t+h} = alpha_i + lambda_t + beta^PS_h shock^PS_i,t + Gamma^PS_h Z_{i,t-1} + epsilon^PS_{i,t+h} for productivity (includes country and year FE); Delta inv^c_{i,t+h} = alpha_i + beta^F_h hat(BAA)_t + Gamma^F_h Z_{i,t-1} + epsilon^F_{i,t+h} for financial (country FE only due to global shock; robustness with year-clustered, two-way clustered and crisis dummies 1998,2009,2020). Controls Z_{i,t-1} = two lags output growth and two lags cyclical inventory changes (robustness one lag). Estimation by OLS with country-clustered SE; productivity also robust estimator per Hamilton 1991/Rousseeuw 1987 to reduce outlier influence. Estimate separately by country group.",
  "Step 7 - Compute LP-based variance share VS^j_{g,h} = 100 * beta^{j2}_{g,h} Var_w(tilde s^j_{i,t}) / Var_w(tilde Delta inv^c_{i,t+h}) where tilde denotes residualized with respect to baseline controls and FEs, Var_w weighted variance using robust weights for productivity and LP-IV sample for financial. Not VAR FEVD, not required to sum to 100%.",
  "Parameters set by authors: H=5, p=1 in VECM, 2 lags controls in LP baseline, world GDP >2% threshold for large economies, median absolute Delta INV/GDP <1% benchmark, 30 annual global observations for BAA first stage, Tbar=28 after differencing/lagging."
 ],
